{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Remove the foreign gods.</b> The meaning is: since Jacob wished to journey from that place, he was concerned lest they take in hand some of the gods of the nations and they be in their possession; and since those of the nations who could have annulled them were not with them before they came into their hand, they have no annulment at the hands of Israel." "And if you say: why did the wives of the men of Shechem not annul them before they came into their hand, as they said of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 43a) — a gentile annuls idolatry even involuntarily and even idolatry belonging to another? It may be answered that the women have the legal status of minors, and minors do not annul idolatry, as we say in tractate Avodah Zarah folio 43, where they said, and this is their language: ״ושמע מינה היודע בטיב וכו׳״ — and one who does not know the nature of idolatry and its service does not annul it — end quote; and the women have the status of a minor. <b>Or</b> it is possible that they were not scrupulous to prevent them from reaching their hand until they had annulled them. Or perhaps the idolatry was hidden among the utensils and the like, and was not noticeable to them to effect its annulment before it reached their hand, and concerning this Jacob commanded to search, to remove the impurity. Or perhaps idolatry or objects used in the service of idolatry had become intermingced and had not been annulled, and everything was interdicted." "And he told them to purify themselves from the contamination of carrion, for idolatry defiles a person and utensils. And his saying <b>and change</b> etc. follows what the Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of the Laws of the Sources of Impurity, and this is his language: every father of impurity that defiles by contact and by carrying, whether a father etc. — a person who touches it or carries it defiles garments at the time of his contact etc. — end quote. Therefore he told them that even if the garments had not touched the idolatry, they are nevertheless impure and need to be changed. And therefore the verse is precise in saying <b>your garments</b> — meaning, those that were on them at the time they plundered the spoils, in which there was idolatry." "And if you say: why be concerned about changing garments, when a person and utensils receive impurity only from a father of impurity, and these garments, even if they touched the idolatry itself, do not become a father of impurity and do not defile a person? It would appear: since we find that Scripture applies the name ״dead״ to idolatry, as it is written (Psalms 106:28) ״ויאכלו זבחי מתים״, and we find that the Torah said (Chullin 3a) that a sword is like a corpse, and the Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of the Laws of the Impurity of a Corpse, and this is his language: ״והוא הדין לשאר כלים וכו׳ ובגדים״ — and the same applies to other utensils etc. and garments — end quote; if so, the garments became like the impurity of the idolatry itself, and therefore the righteous one commanded to change the garments."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:15.197235850Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}